---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import homeFirst from '../assets/hairness-home-first.webp';
import targetFirst from '../assets/hairness-target-first.webp';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Home-first | Hairness"
	description="Why agentic work needs an environment owned independently from providers and product repositories."
	path="/home-first/"
>
	<SiteHeader />
	<main>
		<header class="learn-hero shell">
			<p class="eyebrow">Learn · Home-first</p>
			<h1>Your repository is a Target. <br />It is not a Home.</h1>
			<p>
				Home-first gives the agentic environment its own owner, source and
				lifecycle, independent from every provider and repository it serves.
			</p>
		</header>

		<article class="learn-content shell">
			<section>
				<p class="section-index">01 · The accidental model</p>
				<h2>Target-first makes every repository rebuild the environment.</h2>
				<p>
					Most agent sessions begin inside a product repository. Local
					instructions, skills, methods, memories and provider configuration
					accumulate there because the session needs somewhere to put them.
					The Target becomes an accidental Home.
				</p>
				<div class="topology">Provider → agent inside one repository → context rebuilt → work</div>
				<Image
					class="learn-illustration"
					src={targetFirst}
					alt="Three separate repositories each contain their own provider session and copied agent methods."
					widths={[640, 1000, 1400]}
					sizes="(max-width: 760px) 94vw, 1000px"
				/>
			</section>

			<section>
				<p class="section-index">02 · The shift</p>
				<h2>Home-first gives the environment an independent place to live.</h2>
				<p>
					Providers bring agents to a Front Door. The Home supplies stable
					orientation, the active Desk and reusable Assets. Explicit Bindings
					reach independent Targets without installing Hairness inside them.
				</p>
				<div class="topology topology-home">Provider → Front Door → Home + Desk + Assets → Binding → Target</div>
				<Image
					class="learn-illustration"
					src={homeFirst}
					alt="A source-owned Home contains a Front Door, personal Desks, reusable Assets and governed Artifacts connected to independent Targets through Bindings."
					widths={[640, 1000, 1400]}
					sizes="(max-width: 760px) 94vw, 1000px"
					loading="lazy"
				/>
			</section>

			<section>
				<p class="section-index">03 · The ownership law</p>
				<h2>A thing should not be owned by the system that only hosts, runs or consumes it.</h2>
				<div class="separation-grid">
					<div><code>Home ≠ Target</code><p>The environment is not a product repository.</p></div>
					<div><code>Asset ≠ Artifact</code><p>Equipment is not an outcome of work.</p></div>
					<div><code>Capability ≠ Skill</code><p>A model entrypoint is not the whole product.</p></div>
					<div><code>Source ≠ Projection</code><p>Generated provider files are not canonical.</p></div>
					<div><code>Provider ≠ Owner</code><p>The session host does not own the environment.</p></div>
					<div><code>Conversation ≠ Memory</code><p>A chat is not durable, governed continuity.</p></div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section>
				<p class="section-index">04 · What becomes unacceptable</p>
				<h2>A paradigm is also defined by what it refuses.</h2>
				<ul class="doctrine-list">
					<li>Turning every Target into a store for skills, workflows and personal continuity.</li>
					<li>Maintaining generated provider files as canonical sources.</li>
					<li>Treating a Skill as the complete abstraction for a reusable capability.</li>
					<li>Letting one proprietary client become indispensable to the environment.</li>
					<li>Promoting session output into reusable equipment without human curation.</li>
					<li>Mixing private continuity, shared material and product documents under one owner.</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section>
				<p class="section-index">05 · Framework and paradigm</p>
				<h2>Hairness is the framework. Home-first is the wider model.</h2>
				<p>
					Hairness provides a working implementation of Home-first contracts:
					Homes, Desks, Assets, Artifacts, Targets, Bindings, Projections and a
					Front Door. The paradigm is deliberately larger than one framework.
					Other systems may one day implement Home-first with different
					contracts and experiences.
				</p>
				<blockquote>
					A provider can host the agent. It shouldn’t own the Home.
				</blockquote>
			</section>

			<div class="learn-next">
				<p class="eyebrow">Continue</p>
				<h2>What could a Home be built for?</h2>
				<a class="button button-primary" href="/homes/">Explore conceptual Homes</a>
			</div>
		</article>
	</main>
	<SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
